  ('ev_wiki_herman-melville_1847_38', 'ent_herman-melville', '1847-03', 1847, 'narrative', 'narrative',
   'The two corresponded until 1863, and in his final years Melville "traced and successfully located his old friend" for a further meeting of the two.[80] In March 1847, Omoo, a sequel to Typee, was publ', 'The two corresponded until 1863, and in his final years Melville "traced and successfully located his old friend" for a further meeting of the two.[80] In March 1847, Omoo, a sequel to Typee, was published by Murray in London, and in May by Harper in New York.[72] Omoo is "a slighter but more professional book," according to Milder.[81] Typee and Omoo gave Melville overnight renown as a writer and adventurer, and he often entertained by telling stories to his admirers.', 'src_en_wikipedia',
