   'He confidently set high prices and turned down unsatisfactory sitters.[31] He mentored his friend Emil Fuchs, who was learning to paint portraits in oils.[32]  Works   See also: List of works by John ', 'He confidently set high prices and turned down unsatisfactory sitters.[31] He mentored his friend Emil Fuchs, who was learning to paint portraits in oils.[32]  Works   See also: List of works by John Singer Sargent  Portraits  Nineteenth-century portraits  In the early 1880s, Sargent regularly exhibited portraits at the Salon, and these were mostly full-length portrayals of women, such as Madame Edouard Pailleron (1880) (done en plein-air) and Madame Ramón Subercaseaux (1881)', 'src_en_wikipedia',
