   'A United Press report in 1949 indicated that winds were to blame,[19] while the Los Angeles Times said that the H was destroyed by "vandals or windstorms."[20]  In 1949, the sign drew complaints from ', 'A United Press report in 1949 indicated that winds were to blame,[19] while the Los Angeles Times said that the H was destroyed by "vandals or windstorms."[20]  In 1949, the sign drew complaints from local residents, who called it an "eyesore and detriment to the community" and advocated its demolition.', 'src_en_wikipedia',
