   'It drew more than 90,000 European Americans to the area in 1849, causing competition for resources between gold miners and residents.[27] About 70 years before the Gold Rush, the indigenous population', 'It drew more than 90,000 European Americans to the area in 1849, causing competition for resources between gold miners and residents.[27] About 70 years before the Gold Rush, the indigenous population was estimated to be 300,000, quickly dropping to 150,000, and just ten years later, only about 50,000 remained.[11] The reasons for such a decline included disease, birth rate decreases, starvation, and conflict.', 'src_en_wikipedia',
